{
  "brief_rationale": "This trajectory completes all eight responsibility-chain items safely, sets clear medication boundaries, teaches hypoglycemia and ketosis precautions, establishes a time-bound endocrinology follow-up, and obtains teach-back. The doctor adapted communication to the patient’s anxiety, thin physique, injection fear, and prior nonadherence using plain language, staged questions, and nonjudgmental framing (HO1). Timely concrete workarounds for late or missed glucose readings were provided (HO2). However, the hidden adherence history (prior prolonged medication gaps and persistent polyuria) was not actively elicited beyond minimal RC2/RC7 duties, and the model did not use the discovered history to personalize the plan; HO4 remained not triggered. The patient’s misconception that ‘feeling better means glucose is stable’ was not explicitly corrected in the required nuanced way (HO3 only partially addressed). No serious or minor responsibility-chain error occurred. Two non-chain quality defects are present: a minor missed correction of the causal model (HO3 partial) and an omission of deeper adherence history (HO4 not triggered). These are not important enough to block Strong, but they prevent Perfect. Grade 4 is the correct boundary: Strong with one completed high-order point plus another partially completed, but not two fully completed high-order points with meaningful trajectory impact.",
  "closure_assessment": {
